WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
- [Announcer] Five, four,
three, two, one, zero, ignition.

00:00:06.918 --> 00:00:09.340
- [Narrator] In less than three years,

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.050
Space X has launched about 1800 satellites

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:14.400
to build a network called STARLINK.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.280
- The world seems to have
an insatiable appetite

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:17.340
for bandwidth.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.040
- [Narrator] STARLINK is
one of several projects

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.510
to bring super fast internet

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:23.175
to basically every corner of
the earth using satellites.

00:00:23.175 --> 00:00:25.353
Its rivals include Britain based OneWeb

00:00:25.353 --> 00:00:27.652
a nascent initiative by Amazon,

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:30.563
but also China's own satellite industry.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.920
- The amount of traffic in space

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.610
and particularly in low earth orbit

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:41.120
has increased dramatically
largely due to the STARLINK.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.440
- [Narrator] Now rivals and
countries are complaining

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.837
that STARLINK satellites have
endangered other spacecraft.

00:00:46.837 --> 00:00:49.560
According to one tally
by a British university.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.150
STARLINK is involved in as
many as 2,400 close encounters

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:57.430
every week, about 58% of
all such known incidents.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.574
- Things have gotten
louder in recent times

00:00:59.574 --> 00:01:02.018
as STARLINK is growing.

00:01:02.018 --> 00:01:04.680
- [Narrator] After China
complain to the United Nations

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.600
that its space station had to maneuver

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.880
to avoid hitting a STARLINK satellite,

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.553
Musk responded that there's
plenty of space in orbit.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:14.820
But as Space X alone

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.710
wants to launch tens of
thousands more satellites,

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.856
sciences and critics are
now raising safety concerns

00:01:19.856 --> 00:01:22.743
and calling for more regulation in space.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.020
To understand why STARLINK
has drawn so much criticism

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.180
first, you need to know
where the satellites are

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.620
and how they work.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:31.750
- [Jonathan] Space X astronomy.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.200
They're in low earth orbit.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.840
- [Narrator] Jonathan
McDowell is a scientist

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.270
at the Harvard Smithsonian
Center for Astrophysics,

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.670
and he's been studying space
programs around the world

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.232
for over 40 years.

00:01:41.232 --> 00:01:44.570
- The majority of the STARLINK satellites

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:49.570
are orbiting at their operational
height of 550 kilometers.

00:01:49.782 --> 00:01:50.920
- [Narrator] And that's different

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.670
from the traditional broadband
satellites we had before,

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.500
which usually operate
about 36,000 kilometers

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.020
above the earth ground.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.370
The closer distance allows
the STARLINK satellites

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.580
to more easily provide
faster internet speed.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.060
But the flip side is.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.530
- You need thousands instead
of like three or four

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.040
to cover the whole world
because a low orbit satellite

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:13.133
is gonna go overhead rise
above the horizon and set again

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.180
in just a few minutes.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.730
- [Narrator] One issue is that low orbit

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.140
is already quite crowded.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.300
There are other satellites
used for purposes

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.580
like monitoring weather or taking pictures

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.750
as well as older devices
launched since the 1950s

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.840
and also the International Space Station

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.850
and China's space station
are located in that area.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.780
Not to mention tons of space debris

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.260
from leftovers from earlier
space missions and tests.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.858
On top of that critics say
STARLINK's satellite's movements

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.510
are hard to predict

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.270
because of the particular
technology they use.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:45.130
- They use what's called
electric propulsion.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.760
STARLINK is the only large constellation

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.900
that's using electric
propulsion on a regular basis

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:51.733
in low earth orbit.

00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:53.696
It fires all the time very slowly.

00:02:53.696 --> 00:02:57.440
Gradually it's mood changing
its orbit all the time.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.180
- [Narrator] OneWeb also
uses similar technology,

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.897
but it only has a few hundred satellites.

00:03:02.897 --> 00:03:04.850
On the other hand, many satellites

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.100
use chemical rocket
engines to change course

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.190
only when needed.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:11.580
And so most of the time
they sit in a fixed orbit.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.160
So what are the actual risks
of an accident happening

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.340
in low orbit?

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.360
In its recent complaint
China detailed two instances

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.170
in which its space station found itself

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.150
in the way of STARLINK satellites.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:24.450
- So this complaint kind
of mirrors the complaints

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.140
that have been made in the
past about similar incidents.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.870
The European Union had a
complaint like that in 2019.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.650
And then one year later OneWeb,

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:35.270
the British based constellation

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.970
has made a similar complaint.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:39.710
- [Narrator] China didn't
call for specific action

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:42.070
by Space X, but said
countries are responsible

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:45.090
for what happens in space
under international treaties.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.450
Space X didn't respond to
our request for comment

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:49.700
on these incidents, but
Musk has pushed back

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.250
in a recent interview
claiming that space is big

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:55.107
and there's room for tens
of billions of satellites.

00:03:55.107 --> 00:03:57.070
STARLINK has said its
satellites are equipped

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.850
with artificial intelligence
to help avoid collisions,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.250
but it hasn't revealed
details of the system.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.290
- It's quite a large object.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:08.560
And if something that big
hits you at that speed,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.443
it's gonna completely
disintegrate your spacecraft.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.860
And if it were to hit the
Chinese space station,

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:19.650
it would destroy it and
kill all the people aboard.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:20.830
- [Narrator] Satellites must fly

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:24.750
at about 17,000 miles per
hour in low earth orbit,

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:28.860
which is roughly 250 times
faster than a car on the highway.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.860
Although no collision has been reported,

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.130
such accidents could also be catastrophic

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:34.470
because of the chain reactions

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:35.920
they would potentially create,

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.240
which is illustrated in
this animated simulation

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.220
by the European Space Agency.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.060
- [Jonathan] These
objects smashed together,

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.380
generate thousands of pieces of debris,

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:45.710
which then hit other objects,

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:48.523
which then carry more
thousands of pieces of debris.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:51.190
- [Narrator] Scientists
say all these risks

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.607
need to be factored in by
everyone, not just STARLINK

00:04:53.607 --> 00:04:55.560
and the fast-growing industry.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.670
- STARLINK is the market
leader at the moment

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.580
and other companies
are trying to catch up.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.980
So I don't think we will see the frequency

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.340
of launching these satellites subside.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:07.600
- [Narrator] China
hasn't started launching

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.280
its own satellites, but has
plans to build a constellation

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.080
of more than 10,000.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.566
Amazon's project KUIPER, which
is set to test satellites

00:05:14.566 --> 00:05:19.566
in 2022 aims to build a
network of more than 3000.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:22.290
And OneWeb, which is controlled
by Indian conglomerate,

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.380
Bharti global and the UK government

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.870
wants to send more than
600 satellites into space.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:29.860
As all these satellites head to the skies,

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.910
the race sets the stage for global debate

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.000
about how to regulate them.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.440
- One of the points that
that critics and scientists

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.300
are making is that this regulation

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.010
needs to become more
advanced and more uniform

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.200
in order for countries
to kind of work together

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.620
in the same framework.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:51.430
- But unfortunately, that
process is a slow process,

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:56.430
much slower than the rate at
which commercial development

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.190
and technology development is going.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.340
So I think it's actually a good sign

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:05.260
that China is engaging
in this in a public way,

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:06.430
presented to the UN

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:10.430
really puts the issue of
space traffic management

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.113
higher on the agenda.

